---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Signup from '../components/Signup.astro';
import {CHANNEL_URL} from '../lib/channel';
import {DONATION_METHODS, CONTACT_EMAIL} from '../lib/support';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const GITHUB = 'https://github.com/code-with-sam-dev';
---

<Base
  title="Support - Code with Sam"
  description="Ways to support Code with Sam, most of which cost nothing."
>
  <h1>Support the channel</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every episode takes real work to research, verify and put together: the
    explanation has to be correct, the code has to actually run, and the whole
    thing has to be clear enough to be worth your time. All of it is free, with
    no paywall and no gated newsletter. If it has saved you time, here is how to
    help it keep going.
  </p>

  <h2>Free, and worth more than you would think</h2>
  <p>
    None of these cost anything. All of them matter more to a channel this
    small than a one-off contribution does.
  </p>

  <ul class="support-list">
    <li>
      <span class="n">1</span>
      <div>
        <h3><a href={CHANNEL_URL} rel="noopener">Subscribe on YouTube</a></h3>
        <p>
          Subscriber count is the single number that decides whether YouTube
          shows an episode to anyone who is not already looking for it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="n">2</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Watch to the end, and comment</h3>
        <p>
          Watch time and comments are what the algorithm reads as "this was
          worth someone's afternoon". A comment saying where you have seen the
          problem in production is genuinely useful to me as well, because it
          tells me what to make next.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="n">3</span>
      <div>
        <h3><a href={GITHUB} rel="noopener">Star the repositories</a></h3>
        <p>
          The code is the part that separates this from every other
          explanation. Stars are how other engineers find it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="n">4</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Send one episode to one person</h3>
        <p>
          A colleague preparing for an interview, or the teammate who wrote the
          consumer that broke ordering. One share to the right person beats a
          hundred impressions to the wrong ones.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>

  {
    DONATION_METHODS.length > 0 ? (
      <>
        <h2>Contribute directly</h2>
        <p>
          Entirely optional, and nothing on this site is ever locked behind it.
        </p>
        <div class="cards">
          {DONATION_METHODS.map((m) => (
            <a class="card" href={m.url} rel="noopener">
              <h3>{m.name}</h3>
              <p>{m.note}</p>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </>
    ) : (
      <>
        <h2>Contributing directly</h2>
        <p>
          Not open yet, and I would rather say so than put up a button that
          does not work. Zimbabwe is not a supported merchant country on Stripe,
          which rules out several of the usual options, so I am working out a
          route that actually pays out before asking anyone for money.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want to support the work in the meantime, the four things above
          genuinely help more at this stage. If you represent a company and want
          to talk about sponsorship, that is <a href={`${base}/sponsor`}>a
          separate page</a>.
        </p>
      </>
    )
  }

  <h2>Hire me, or work with me</h2>
  <p>
    I am a software engineer, and the repositories on this site are a fair
    sample of how I build things. For consulting, contract work or sponsorship,
    email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>

  <Signup compact />
</Base>
